```c
// STACK — automatic storage, freed when function returns
void stack_example(void) {
    int local = 42;      // on stack, freed automatically
    int arr[100];        // fixed size, on stack
}  // local and arr are gone here

// HEAP — dynamic storage, must be freed manually
void heap_example(void) {
    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof(int));  // on heap
    if (!p) { perror("malloc"); exit(1); }
    p[0] = 42;
    free(p);   // must free manually
}  // p (the pointer variable) is gone, but the heap memory was freed
```

| | Stack | Heap |
|---|---|---|
| Allocation | Automatic | Manual (`malloc`/`calloc`) |
| Deallocation | Automatic | Manual (`free`) |
| Size | Fixed at compile time | Dynamic |
| Speed | Very fast | Slower (bookkeeping) |
| Limit | Small (typically ~1–8 MB) | Large (limited by RAM) |
| Lifetime | Until function returns | Until `free` is called |

