A **dangling pointer** points to memory that has been freed or gone out of scope:

```c
// Dangling pointer after free
int *p = malloc(sizeof(int));
*p = 42;
free(p);
// p is now dangling — the memory may be reused by the OS
*p = 100;  // UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR

// Fix: set to NULL after freeing
free(p);
p = NULL;
if (p != NULL) *p = 100;  // safe — NULL check prevents use

// Dangling pointer to local variable
int *bad_ptr;
{
    int local = 42;
    bad_ptr = &local;
}  // local is destroyed here
// bad_ptr now points to invalid stack memory
```

Prevention:
1. Set pointers to `NULL` after `free`
2. Never return pointers to local variables
3. Use tools: **Valgrind** (memory error detector), **AddressSanitizer** (`-fsanitize=address`)
